Psoriasis
Young Adult & Adolecence
Well-demarcated erythematous plaques, Silvery-white scale, Auspitz sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of generalized skin plaques causing unrelenting localized soreness, severe pruritus, and inability to sleep or wear clothing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a rapidly spreading, painful, and intensely itchy skin eruption covering the majority of the trunk and limbs, causing severe sleep deprivation and functional impairment. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was mandated to rule out an acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP) or a severe secondary bacterial infection, and to provide immediate, high-potency topical relief and systemic symptom control prior to the next regular GP session.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is covered in these thick, red, burning scales that itch constantly, and if I scratch them, they bleed everywhere."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe acute flare of a known, previously mild skin condition. Over the past two weeks, following an episode of intense emotional stress, the patient has developed rapidly expanding, well-demarcated erythematous plaques. These are thickly covered with a characteristic silvery-white scale. The patient notes severe pruritus and localized skin soreness. The lesions are symmetrically distributed, primarily involving the extensor surfaces of the knees and elbows, but have now generalized to the trunk. The patient reports the Koebner phenomenon, noting new lesions developing exactly where the waistband of their trousers rubs. The intense itch and soreness have resulted in total sleep deprivation for the past 48 hours.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely fatigued and unable to wear standard clothing due to friction pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychosocial distress regarding the visible nature of the flare; extreme exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, anxious, and is constantly adjusting their loose clothing to avoid skin contact.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals extensive, symmetrical, bilateral presentation of well-demarcated erythematous plaques with prominent silvery-white scaling. Gentle removal of a scale scale elicits the Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding). The scalp demonstrates thick, hyperkeratotic plaques extending beyond the anterior hairline. Nail examination reveals distinct pitting and early onycholysis. There is no evidence of frank pustules, systemic toxicity, or spreading cellulitic erythema. Joint examination is negative for asymmetric oligoarthritis. The clinical presentation firmly satisfies the diagnostic tetrad of well-demarcated erythematous plaques, silvery-white scale, and a positive Auspitz sign.
Environmental Observations: Bed sheets are visibly speckled with small blood spots from nocturnal scratching (Auspitz bleeding).
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been applying a low-potency over-the-counter moisturizer with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Flare of Chronic Plaque Psoriasis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tinea Corporis (Ringworm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of thick, silvery scales, the classic extensor distribution, and the positive Auspitz sign, whereas tinea typically presents with a clear center and an active, advancing scaly border.

Nummular Eczema - Ruled out/unlikely due to the thick, adherent silvery scale and nail pitting, which are hallmarks of psoriasis, rather than the intensely weeping, coin-shaped lesions of eczema.

Seborrhoeic Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of lesions on the elbows/knees and the thick silvery scale, whereas seborrhoeic dermatitis typically features greasy, yellowish scales confined to the scalp, nasolabial folds, and chest.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, generalized flare of plaque psoriasis complicated by unrelenting pruritus, localized pain, and severe sleep deprivation. The rapid spread of the plaques and the severe functional impairment necessitated an immediate after-hours evaluation. Delaying assessment would have resulted in continued severe distress, further sleep loss, and extensive traumatic excoriation of the skin. Immediate clinical examination safely excluded a generalized infectious or drug-induced eruption and allowed for the prompt initiation of potent topical corticosteroids to rapidly reduce the intense inflammation, alongside a sedating antihistamine to physically break the acute itch-scratch cycle overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment (high-potency corticosteroid) to be applied directly to the active plaques twice daily. Administered Promethazine 25mg PO stat to provide immediate sedation and suppress the severe nocturnal pruritus. Recommended formal dermatology referral via the regular GP for consideration of systemic or biologic therapy given the extensive body surface area involvement.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically soften the thick plaques and increase the absorption of the steroid ointment, soak in a lukewarm bath containing an emollient oil for 15 minutes before application, directly breaking down the hyperkeratotic barrier. To prevent triggering the Koebner phenomenon, wear only loose, seamless 100% cotton clothing, actively removing the mechanical friction that causes new psoriasis plaques to form.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to monitor the initial response to the high-potency topical therapy and initiate a specialist dermatology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the skin suddenly becomes generalized bright red, feels hot all over the body, and the patient develops shivering, a high fever, or fluid-filled blisters over the plaques.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a severe flare of a chronic autoimmune skin condition; long-term management requires specialized dermatological care.
Clinical Course: The potent steroid ointment will significantly reduce the redness, scaling, and soreness within 4 to 7 days, while the antihistamine will facilitate immediate sleep.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if your skin becomes intensely hot and bright red all over your body, or if you develop a high fever and uncontrollable shivering.

